Q: Add Reference option missing from Solution Explorer A: <p>Late to the party, and (disclaimer!) I'm a n00b with Visual Studio.</p>
<p>I had the exact same problem in VS 2019 community. What worked for me: I knew my project needed 3 specific dll's. I found an XML file with the extension .csproj, that listed them something like this:</p>
<pre><code>    &lt;Reference Include=&quot;OEM.Database&gt;
      &lt;SpecificVersion&gt;False&lt;/SpecificVersion&gt;
      &lt;HintPath&gt;..\..\myname\this_other_app_that_has_em\OEM.Database.dll&lt;/HintPath&gt;
    &lt;/Reference&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>I just copied the dll's into the directory with that .csproj, stripped the path leaving the filename, and that fixed it for me.</p>

Q: When to use Transport.send() vs. sendMessage() A: <p>Yes, there is an implementation difference that is not documented very clearly in the FAQ. The main difference is that with <code>transport.sendMessage(...)</code> you manage its lifecycle and have to invoke <code>saveChanges()</code>. The FAQ states <code>Transport.send()</code> does this, but it doesn't clearly state you have to do it yourself if you use <code>transport.sendMessage()</code>. The JavaDoc is more clear about this.</p>
<p>However, <strong>no, it's not safe to keep a transport open for a very long time</strong>, they tend to close/fail. This depends on network, proxies, firewalls, SMTP server etc. and is entirely environment specific.  Angus' <code>SMTPTransport.isConnected()</code> can probe the server using <code>NOOP</code>, but even that cannot guarantee that the connection will still be alive for the next send.</p>
<p>The usual solution is to reuse connections during bursts (like you suggest, but with a connection pool rather than a single <code>Transport</code> instance), let idle connections expire, and create  new ones on demand (or auto-create when one expires/fails). This is exactly what <a href="https://simplejavamail.org/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Simple Java Mail</a> does in its <a href="https://simplejavamail.org/features.html#section-sending-asynchronously" rel="nofollow noreferrer">batch-module</a> (disclaimer: I maintain this open source project).</p>
<p>But it can be simple: If you already know the batch, Simple Java Mail can send it sequentially over one connection without using a connection pool:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>// use a permanent mailer, or create one on the fly:
try (Mailer mailer = MailerBuilder
        .withSMTPServer(&quot;smtp.example.com&quot;, 587, &quot;user&quot;, &quot;secret&quot;)
        .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
        .buildMailer()) {

    mailer.sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails, false);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Exactly your use case: open one Transport, send every email through it, and close it when the batch completes or fails.</p>
<p>If you have a more dynamic runtime batch, there's also:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>mailer.withOpenConnection(sender -&gt; { // single Transport instance
    while (database.hasPendingMail()) {
        PendingMail pending = database.nextPendingMail();

        sender.sendMail(pending.toEmail());
        database.markAsSent(pending);
    }
});
</code></pre>
<p>But this version also shouldn't take hours or you'll run into the same issue.</p>
<p>If you need responsiveness and process heavy loads with bursts over longer periods of time, you can use the batch-module. Simply including it as dependency already works, but you can configure it as required:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Mailer mailer = MailerBuilder
        .withSMTPServer(&quot;smtp.example.com&quot;, 587, &quot;user&quot;, &quot;secret&quot;)
        .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
        .withConnectionPoolCoreSize(4) // default 0
        .withConnectionPoolMaxSize(4) // default 4
        .withConnectionPoolClaimTimeoutMillis(30_000) // default 'never' (actualy 25 days)
        .withConnectionPoolExpireAfterMillis(30_000) // default 5 seconds
        .async()
        .buildMailer();

// whenever an incoming message requires a response:
CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt; result = mailer.sendMail(email);
</code></pre>
<p>So now you're not only reusing connections, you have them standing by at all times, and you don't have to worry about calling <code>saveChanges()</code>. You can read more about it in the documentation on <a href="https://www.simplejavamail.org/configuration.html#section-batch-and-clustering" rel="nofollow noreferrer">batch configuration</a> page.</p>
<p>For even heavier workloads, you can configure (multiple) <em>clusters</em> of SMTP servers and have a dedicated connection pool for each.</p>
<p>There are setups without Simple Java Mail as well as without the batch-module. In these cases you can use the underlying <a href="https://github.com/simple-java-mail/smtp-connection-pool" rel="nofollow noreferrer">smtp-connection-pool</a> directly, which recently had major overhaul. Read more about these on the <a href="https://www.simplejavamail.org/smtp-connection-pooling.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">batch orchestration</a> page.</p>

Q: I&#39;m trying to implement compile-time recursive function calls using if constexpr A: <p>You missed <code>else</code>. Otherwise, the code after the <code>if constexpr</code> statement is not excluded, and the recursion is infinite.</p>
<pre><code>template &lt;size_t N&gt;
void rec()
{
  if constexpr (N == 0) return;
  else {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; N &lt;&lt; std::endl;
    rec&lt;N - 1&gt;();
  }
}
</code></pre>

Q: YouTube iframe always overlays the video for ~3 seconds before disappearing A: <h1>Removing YouTube's iframe chrome by scaling the player, not the frame</h1>
<p>Short version: YouTube's player chrome is sized in CSS pixels, and the video is<br />
sized in percentages. So they do not scale together. Mount the iframe enormous,<br />
scale it back down with a CSS transform, and the chrome shrinks into nothing<br />
while the video still fills your box.</p>
<h1>The code</h1>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class=&quot;stage&quot;&gt;
  &lt;iframe
    class=&quot;player&quot;
    src=&quot;https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/VIDEO_ID?enablejsapi=1&amp;controls=0&amp;mute=1&amp;playsinline=1&amp;rel=0&amp;iv_load_policy=3&amp;disablekb=1&amp;fs=0&amp;cc_load_policy=0&quot;
    allow=&quot;autoplay; encrypted-media&quot;
    title=&quot;&quot;
  &gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.stage {
  position: relative;
  width: 360px;          /* whatever you actually want */
  height: 203px;
  overflow: hidden;      /* load-bearing: the overflow must be clipped */
  background: #000;
}

.player {
  --scale: 200;          /* how much bigger the player is mounted */
  --overscan: 1.12;      /* cover instead of fit; see below */

  position: absolute;
  border: 0;

  /* Percentages, not pixels, so the factor holds at any container size:
     20000% of the box, scaled by 1/200, lands back on the box. */
  width: calc(var(--scale) * var(--overscan) * 100%);
  height: calc(var(--scale) * var(--overscan) * 100%);

  /* Re-centre the overflow, half on each side. */
  left: calc((1 - var(--overscan)) / 2 * 100%);
  top: calc((1 - var(--overscan)) / 2 * 100%);

  transform: scale(calc(1 / var(--scale)));
  transform-origin: 0 0; /* top-left, or the reduction re-centres and shifts */
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// Subscribe first — the embed stays silent until it is asked to talk.
setInterval(() =&gt; {
  iframe.contentWindow.postMessage(
    JSON.stringify({ event: &quot;listening&quot;, id: 1, channel: &quot;widget&quot; }),
    &quot;https://www.youtube-nocookie.com&quot;
  );
}, 150);

let rewound = false;
addEventListener(&quot;message&quot;, (e) =&gt; {
  if (e.origin !== &quot;https://www.youtube-nocookie.com&quot;) return;
  let data;
  try { data = JSON.parse(e.data); } catch { return; }

  const at = data?.info?.currentTime;
  if (typeof at !== &quot;number&quot;) return;

  // Two halves. The clock must first be seen at the START of this video —
  // otherwise, if you ever swap videos with loadVideoById, the previous one is
  // still reporting a large currentTime and answers for the new one instantly.
  if (at &lt; 0.5) { rewound = true; return; }
  if (rewound) stage.style.opacity = &quot;1&quot;;   // real frames are going past
});
</code></pre>
<p>Keep the stage at <code>opacity: 0</code> until then, with a poster or a still image<br />
underneath, and add a timeout that reveals anyway — a preview stuck behind a<br />
banner because one <code>postMessage</code> went missing is a worse bug than a brief ugly<br />
frame.</p>
